  "response": "\"이상한 건가요\"라고 물으셨는데, 그 질문 자체가 꽤 무겁게 느껴집니다. 가족에게서 나쁜 사람이라는 말을 듣고, 며칠째 딸이 문을 닫고, 남편까지 같은 말을 하는데, 그 와중에 혼자 앉아 '내가 언제 설레본 적이 있었나'를 떠올려보고 계신 거잖아요. 그건 이상한 게 아니라, 자기 자신을 아주 정직하게 들여다보고 계신 거예요.\n\n가족 입장에서는 축하를 못 받는 것처럼 느껴졌을 수 있고, 내담자 입장에서는 걱정하는 게 사랑의 방식이었을 수 있어요. 그 둘이 부딪혀서 다들 상처를 받고 있는 것 같습니다.\n\n그런데 내담자 말씀 중에 한 문장이 계속 남습니다. \"뭔가를 기대하고 설레본 적이 있나 싶어서요, 그게 잘 안 떠오르더라고요.\" 가족 갈등보다 이 부분이 더 오래된 이야기 같다는 느낌이 드는데, 이쪽을 좀 더 함께 살펴봐도 괜찮을까요?",
  "reasoning": "Let me analyze this carefully.\n\nThe client is sharing a complex emotional situation:\n1. Her daughter got accepted for an exchange program, but instead of celebrating, she immediately worried about dangers, language issues, etc.\n2. Her daughter said something hurtful: \"You can't tolerate good things happening in my life\" and has been silent for days.\n3. Her husband also said she always finds reasons why things won't work.\n4. She feels misunderstood - she believes she's just trying to protect her family by preparing for worst cases.\n5. But then she reflects: \"When was the last time I felt excited or hopeful about something?\" - and can't remember.\n6. She asks \"Am I weird/strange?\"\n\nKey themes:\n- She's using worry as a form of love/protection, but it's being received as negativity by her family\n- There's a deeper layer here: she can't recall ever feeling excited or hopeful - this suggests a pattern of not allowing herself positive anticipation, possibly rooted in something deeper\n- She's asking \"am I weird?\" - this is a vulnerable self-question that needs careful handling\n- She came to counseling because of this realization about her inability to feel excitement/hope\n\nWhat she needs now:\n- Validation that she's not \"weird\" - but not dismissive reassurance\n- Acknowledgment of the pain in being misunderstood by family\n- Recognition of the deeper insight she's made about not feeling excitement/hope\n- Not to rush into solutions or reframe too quickly\n\nI should:\n1. Not dismiss her question \"am I weird?\" with simple reassurance\n2. Acknowledge the weight of what she shared - the family conflict AND the deeper realization\n3. Honor the insight she made about not remembering feeling excited - this is significant\n4. Maybe gently explore that deeper layer without being too clinical\n\nI want to respond to both layers: the surface (family conflict, feeling misunderstood) and the deeper layer (the inability to feel excitement/hope). The deeper layer seems to be what brought her here and what she's most vulnerable about.\n\nLet me craft a response that:\n- Doesn't say \"you're not weird\" too simply (that would be dismissive)\n- Acknowledges the pain of being seen as the \"negative one\" by family\n- Honors the realization about not feeling excitement\n- Opens space for that deeper exploration\n\nI'll write in natural Korean, conversational counselor tone.\n",
